              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.7: Energy levels in an atom"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.419,
            0.089,
            0.759,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.2.3 Bohr’s model of the atom"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.418,
            0.111,
            0.888,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To explain why atoms are stable, Niels Bohr proposed a new model of the atom in 1913. According to Bohr:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.426,
            0.166,
            0.89,
            0.251
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Electrons do not move randomly around the nucleus but follow fixed circular paths called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "stationary states",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "orbits",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "shells",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In each shell, an electron has a definite amount of energy, so these shells are also called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "energy levels",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.427,
            0.253,
            0.888,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y These shells are represented by the letters K, L, M, N, ... or by the numbers n = 1, 2, 3, 4, ... (Fig. 8.7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.291,
            0.888,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Electrons can revolve only in these allowed shells, and not in between them. While moving in a fixed shell, an electron does not lose energy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.047,
            0.334,
            0.214,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.039,
            0.355,
            0.22,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.039,
                0.355,
                0.22,
                0.479
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.328,
            0.888,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y The first energy level K (n = 1) is the one closest to the nucleus (Fig. 8.7) and has the least energy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.366,
            0.89,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y The energy of these levels increases as we move away from the nucleus. That is, the energy of an electron in the L-shell (n = 2) is more than that of an electron in the K-shell (n = 1). The farther away a shell is from the nucleus, the higher is its energy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.437,
            0.889,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "An electron can move to another shell by absorbing or releasing a fixed amount of energy equal to the difference between the energies of the two levels."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.049,
            0.483,
            0.207,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Niels Bohr",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " was a professor of physics at Copenhagen University, Denmark. He was curious about how atoms exist because the old models could not explain why electrons stay around the nucleus without collapsing. His explanation of the atomic structure provided more clarity. Niels Bohr received the Nobel Prize in 1922, for his work on the structure of the atom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.491,
            0.76,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Each shell can hold only a certain number of electrons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.512,
            0.89,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You may be wondering, how does Bohr’s model explain stability? In Bohr’s model, electrons also move in circular paths around the positively charged nucleus, as in Rutherford’s model. But why don’t they lose energy while doing so? Bohr addressed this by introducing the concept of stationary states as a postulate. In a stationary state, the energy of an electron remains constant, even though it is in motion around the nucleus. Bohr’s model could explain many experimental observations and marked a major step in understanding the atomic structure."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.656,
            0.322,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.259,
                0.656,
                0.322,
                0.687
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_15.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.666,
            0.547,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.697,
            0.745,
            0.714
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why are Bohr’s shells called K, L, M, N... and not, A, B, C, D?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.261,
            0.715,
            0.863,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The naming came from early X‑ray experiments by the physicist, Charles Barkla, who called the first observed X-ray line K. He didn’t start naming from A to leave room for possible discovery of a series earlier than the K series, although none were ever found. Bohr adopted the same notation for atomic shells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.825,
            0.888,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Later, even Bohr’s model was found to have limitations, and yet Next another model, the quantum mechanical model, was proposed. Level You will learn about it in higher grades. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Up",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
